WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.860
"Minneapolis on fire."

00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:02.840
"This is getting serious."

00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:04.620
"Oh, oh, oh my goodness."

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:06.460
You watch the TV news...

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:08.720
"We've got a little bit of a fire 
breaking out on the left here."

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:11.840
...and you're like, oh my goodness, all the protests are riots.

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:12.660
"...vandalizing..."

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:13.880
"Fires have been started..."

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:14.940
"Things got out of control..."

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:17.660
We're not gonna pay attention to the protesters in a city

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:19.420
that has been wracked by this violence...

00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:22.160
"No justice, no peace!"

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:23.960
...who are marching peacefully,

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:25.420
and chanting names.

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:29.960
"George Floyd, George Floyd, George Floyd..."

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:39.140
Which is going to get more
attention? A group of 35 people standing,

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:45.440
chanting names, singing songs, doing the
Cupid Shuffle, as I saw one group of protesters doing,

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:49.880
or, five people on the
ground bleeding and screaming and crying

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:51.220
and throwing rocks?

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:54.920
Our press is driven
by eyeballs, and attention, and clicks,

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:59.920
and advertising, and passion, and raising
the anxiety of viewers.

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:04.040
Dramatic images are money.

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:09.740
They keep eyeballs on the
screen. So they will constantly show action.

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:13.480
You need to understand the
limitations of those visuals.

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:15.020
I'm a college professor.

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:17.940
My doctorate is in political science, I teach in a journalism department.

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:21.000
I would say, across
the board, the coverage has not been very good.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:23.300
It's passing, but it's not very good.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:28.340
And the reason it's not very good is because
there are not enough conversations about

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:30.460
the roots of these protests.

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:34.380
What they are showing you is what is happening right now on the ground.

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:36.720
What those
visuals cannot tell you

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:43.220
is the entire historical context that has led to that
scene on the ground.

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:50.500
And one thing that a lot of mainstream media doesn't do well is give you that full context.

00:01:54.320 --> 00:02:00.440
You can't untangle this moment from
where we are, just in this year.

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:03.520
Starting with February, with the coronavirus...

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:06.380
Ahmaud Arbery's lynching,

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:08.660
having people in shelter in place,

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:13.260
having people become
angry that they are in shelter in place orders,

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:17.360
having the economy
significantly slow down.

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:21.100
There are so many events, just since January,

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:22.780
that led
up to this moment.

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:26.740
Not to mention the hundreds of years before that.

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:29.840
This becomes a very complicated story.

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:36.860
"Come to me right now. This woman just got tased."

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:39.160
Who is to blame for the violence?

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:46.240
It is crazy that when we talk about
destruction of property,

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:50.027
that the presumption is that the unarmed people
who are protesting

00:02:50.027 --> 00:02:52.400
are more likely to cause property destruction

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:55.110
than the
paramilitary cops who are showing up.

00:02:55.110 --> 00:03:00.080
Because tear gas canisters are really
hot, flash bombs can start fires,

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:04.880
tear gas leads people to run and break through
windows in order to hide and get help.

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:08.720
I'm not saying the police are intending
to cause damage.

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:13.600
But if you're randomly shooting out tear gas, it's gonna end up in the back alley,

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:15.720
with a pile of newspapers and some garbage.

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:19.540
Does that mean that there aren't protesters who engage in destruction?

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:20.940
Of course there
are.

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:23.760
To say that that all this is turning into looters

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:26.780
is like saying
everybody at the party is a drunk driver.

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:27.580
No.

00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:29.760
Some people will leave and cause
problems.

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:34.100
But you can't say everybody at the party is the worst-behaving person.

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:39.060
I think one thing that we actually don't do well,

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:45.960
and don't quite understand, is
anti-blackness, and how it works in our society,

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:52.460
how it is in our language,
and how it is used by mainstream media.

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:57.680
We have spent the last month watching
protests who want the country to open up,

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:01.700
and we saw images of these people
screaming, and yelling, and spitting on

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:04.140
police officers, who were plainclothes
officers.

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:05.760
They weren't in riot gear. They weren't in masks.

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:09.420
Now we see protests based on violence and death.

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:11.700
And we see peaceful
protesters come out.

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:13.760
But suddenly, the cops come out in riot gear,

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:18.440
and everything looks like some sort of violent takeover in some 80s post-apocalyptic film.

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:22.380
What I'm reminded of are photos in New Orleans

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:25.120
of white people leaving stores with food,

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:29.000
and the headline being, this is how people are surviving.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:34.160
And black folks leaving the store with food, and calling them looters.

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:45.040
This is about a community that is
treated differently by the police in our country.

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:48.860
Just consuming the news right
now in the next five days

00:04:48.860 --> 00:04:52.980
is not going to give you a good understanding as to
why this is happening.

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:58.860
The emails I get, where people say,
Well, I was in favor of the protesters,

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:01.220
and then they turned violent, and it's a
problem.

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:08.220
Well, why are you more offended by people being violent, in response to state-sponsored violence,

00:05:10.180 --> 00:05:14.940
than you are by the initial act that caused everything?

